                        </p>
                        <p>Otters fall into seven genera. <em>Lutra</em>, <em>Lontra</em>, and <em>Aonyx</em> account for most freshwater encounters; <em>Enhydra</em> (sea otter) is the only fully marine species.</p>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                  <h2>River otters</h2>
                  <p>The dominant freshwater lineage, found across Eurasia and the Americas. Slender bodies, prominent whiskers, partial webbing.</p>
                  <table data-layout="default">
                    <colgroup>
                      <col style="width: 200.0px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 760.0px;"/>
                    </colgroup>
                    <tbody>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Lutra lutra</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>Eurasian otter — Europe, North Africa, much of Asia.</p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Lontra canadensis</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>North American river otter — Canada, US, northern Mexico.</p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Lontra longicaudis</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>Neotropical river otter — Central and South America.</p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                    </tbody>
