              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="fixed-width">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <h2>Pull-quotes</h2>
                  <p>Pull-quotes use the native <code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code> element — styled by <code>richdoc.css</code> with an oversized opening glyph, Fraunces italic, and small-caps attribution via <code>&lt;cite&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>.</p>
                  <blockquote><p>Instead of imagining that our main task is to instruct a computer what to do, let us concentrate rather on explaining to human beings what we want a computer to do.</p> Donald Knuth — Literate Programming, 1984</blockquote>
                  <h2>Comparison tables</h2>
                  <p>For decision matrices. Tones get a coloured dot prefix and a low-opacity stripe — readable in print, distinctive on screen.</p>
                  <table data-layout="default">
                    <colgroup>
                      <col style="width: 243.7px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 253.6px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 219.0px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 243.7px;"/>
                    </colgroup>
                    <thead>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Aspect</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Markdown</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Pure HTML</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>richdoc</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Layout primitives</td>
                        <td>✗ No multi-column, no cards</td>
                        <td>· Possible, verbose</td>
                        <td>✓ Built in</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Authoring vocabulary</td>
                        <td>✓ Tiny, universal</td>
                        <td>✗ Unbounded — drifts</td>
                        <td>✓ Closed, linted</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>LLM authoring reliability</td>
                        <td>· Good</td>
                        <td>✗ Drifts under load</td>
                        <td>✓ Validated at write time</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <td>Render anywhere</td>
                        <td>✓ Yes — GitHub renders it</td>
                        <td>✓ Yes — any browser</td>
                        <td>· Browser only</td>
                      </tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                  <h2>Key/value metadata</h2>
                  <p>Use <code>rd-kv</code> for short status blocks at the top of a doc. No outer box, just hairlines.</p>
                  <table data-layout="default">
                    <colgroup>
                      <col style="width: 200.0px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 760.0px;"/>
                    </colgroup>
                    <tbody>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Owner</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>platform team</p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Status</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>
                            <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1">
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="title">in progress</ac:parameter>
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Yellow</ac:parameter>
                            </ac:structured-macro>
                          </p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Priority</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>
                            <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1">
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="title">P0</ac:parameter>
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Red</ac:parameter>
                            </ac:structured-macro>
                          </p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Tags</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>
                            <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1">
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="title">infra</ac:parameter>
                            </ac:structured-macro>
                            <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1">
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="title">auth</ac:parameter>
                            </ac:structured-macro>
                            <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1">
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="title">security</ac:parameter>
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Blue</ac:parameter>
                            </ac:structured-macro>
                          </p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Notes</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>Values can contain inline HTML, badges, links — anything inline.</p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                  <h2>Code blocks</h2>
                  <p>Code lives in <code>rd-code</code>. The bar at the top shows the language as a tracked small-caps kicker and an optional title in Fraunces italic. Syntax highlighting loads on demand from highlight.js and is themed against the editorial palette — no third-party stylesheet.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">typescript</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">auth.ts</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[import { signToken } from "./jwt.ts";
          import type { TokenPayload } from "./types.ts";
          
          export async function authenticate(payload: TokenPayload): Promise<string> {
          	const key = await loadSigningKey();
          	if (!key) {
          		throw new Error("signing key unavailable");
          	}
          	return signToken(payload, key, { algorithm: "EdDSA" });
          }]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">bash</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[# Initialize a richdoc next to your file
          cd docs/
          richdoc init .
          richdoc new plan.html --template plan]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>Diffs</h2>
                  <p><code>rd-diff</code> renders a unified diff with additions and deletions colour-coded. If <code>lang</code> is set, the line bodies are syntax-highlighted by the same engine that powers <code>rd-code</code>.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">diff</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">auth.ts</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[@@ -3,5 +3,10 @@
           import { signToken } from "./jwt.ts";
           import type { TokenPayload } from "./types.ts";
          
          -export async function authenticate(payload: TokenPayload): Promise<string> {
          -	const key = await loadSigningKey();
          -	return signToken(payload, key, { algorithm: "EdDSA" });
          +export async function authenticate(
          +	payload: TokenPayload,
          +	opts: AuthOptions = {},
          +): Promise<Token> {
          +	const key = await loadSigningKey(opts.keyId);
          +	if (!key) throw new AuthError("signing key unavailable");
          +	return signToken(payload, key, { algorithm: opts.algorithm ?? "EdDSA" });
           }]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>Math</h2>
                  <p>Use <code>rd-math</code> for KaTeX-rendered formulas. Block display by default; pass <code>display="inline"</code> for inline use, e.g. the relation <em>E = mc^2</em> can sit inside a sentence. Block displays render between hairline rules:</p>
                  <p>
                    <em>P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A)\, P(A)}{P(B)}</em>
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <em>\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi(\mathbf{r}, t) = -\frac{i}{\hbar} \hat{H}\, \Psi(\mathbf{r}, t)</em>
                  </p>
                  <h2>Icons</h2>
                  <p>Inline SVG glyphs from the full Lucide library (~1,900 names at the pinned <code>lucide-static</code> version). A core set ships inline in <code>richdoc.js</code>; everything else is lazy-loaded from jsDelivr on first reference and cached. Run <code>richdoc components --tag rd-icon</code> for the authoritative list.</p>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="two_equal" ac:breakout-mode="default">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="panel">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="panel-type">note</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <p>
                          <strong>Status</strong>
                        </p>
                        <p>check  x  warn  info</p>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                </ac:layout-cell>
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="panel">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="panel-type">note</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <p>
                          <strong>Direction</strong>
                        </p>
                        <p>up  down  next  out</p>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="two_equal" ac:breakout-mode="default">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="panel">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="panel-type">note</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <p>
                          <strong>Editorial</strong>
                        </p>
                        <p>sparkles  zap  star  bookmark</p>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                </ac:layout-cell>
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="panel">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="panel-type">info</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <p>
                          <strong>Lazy (CDN)</strong>
                        </p>
                        <p>heart  compass  cloud  database  cpu  package  rocket  telescope</p>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="fixed-width">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <p>Sizes:  small,  medium,  large.</p>
                  <h2>Tabs</h2>
                  <p>Tabbed content for the same idea expressed three ways. First tab is active; arrow keys navigate.</p>
                  <h3>Overview</h3>
                  <p>Tabs are good for the same content rendered three ways: language variants, before/after, examples by stack.</p>
                  <p>They are <strong>not</strong> for hiding important decisions — humans scanning the doc will miss whatever isn't on the active tab.</p>
                  <h3>When to use</h3>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Same example in multiple languages.</li>
                    <li>Before / after code comparisons.</li>
                    <li>Optional deep dives that interrupt the main flow.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <h3>When NOT to use</h3>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Hiding important steps. Use sections instead.</li>
                    <li>Long content. Tabs cap at a screenful of comfortable reading.</li>
                    <li>Search-critical content (only the active tab is visible to Ctrl+F on first load).</li>
                  </ul>
                  <h2>Timeline</h2>
                  <p>Sequenced events: release history, project milestones, incident postmortems. Dates in tracked small-caps, titles in Fraunces italic, the rule is dotted.</p>
                  <ul>
                    <li><strong>2026-05-14</strong> — v0.1 PoC — Initial vocabulary (17 tags), three templates, smoke-tested CLI.</li>
                    <li><strong>2026-05-15</strong> — v0.2 framework refactor — Modular source tree, build pipeline, schema co-located with each component, five new tags.</li>
                    <li><strong>2026-05-16</strong> — v0.3 editorial redesign — Fraunces + Geist + Fira Code, hairline rules, pull-quotes, oversized stats. Five new tags (<code>rd-icon</code>, <code>rd-math</code>, <code>rd-diff</code>, glossary support via <code>rd-kv layout="stacked"</code>); <code>rd-code</code> upgraded with syntax highlighting and line numbers.</li>
                    <li><strong>TBD</strong> — v1.0 multi-doc sites — <code>richdoc site</code> for static collections with sidebar nav and search.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <h2>Diagrams</h2>
                  <p>One element, ~25 diagram languages. Source is sent to a Kroki-compatible endpoint (default <code>kroki.io</code>) and the rendered SVG embeds inline. Set <code>endpoint</code> per-element or <code>&lt;rd-page diagram-endpoint="…"&gt;</code> for the whole doc; for sensitive content point at a self-hosted Kroki. Hover any diagram and click the corner <strong>maximize</strong> button for the fullscreen pan/zoom viewer (0 fit, 1 reset, arrow keys pan, Esc close).</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">mermaid</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[flowchart LR
          	A[Agent writes .html] --> B{richdoc lint}
          	B -- OK --> C[Open in browser]
          	B -- errors --> D[Fix and re-run]
          	D --> B
          	C --> E[Human review]]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">plantuml</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[@startuml
          actor Agent
          participant "richdoc CLI" as CLI
          database Browser
          Agent -> CLI : richdoc lint doc.html
          CLI --> Agent : ok
          Agent -> Browser : open doc.html
          Browser --> Agent : rendered page
          @enduml]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">d2</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[Browser -> API: POST /login
          API -> DB: verify
          API -> Browser: set cookie]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>Figures</h2>
                  <p>Wrap an image, SVG, or diagram with a centred caption. The caption is set in Fraunces italic with a "Fig." kicker in the accent colour.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">mermaid</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[flowchart TB
          	Plan --> Draft --> Validate --> Publish
          	Validate -- fail --> Draft]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <p>
                    <em>Document lifecycle: author, validate, render, review.</em>
                  </p>
                  <h2>Collapsible details</h2>
                  <p>Use <code>rd-detail</code> for content that is relevant but not on the main path — appendices, full error logs, derivations. Native <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> under the hood, so it works without JS and remains keyboard-accessible.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="expand">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">Why a fixed vocabulary instead of free-form HTML?</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:rich-text-body>
                      <p>Free-form HTML lets agents drift. One doc invents <code>&lt;callout&gt;</code>, the next uses <code>&lt;note&gt;</code>, the third leaves it as bold-italic prose. The reader experience fragments and the agent has no way to verify their output.</p>
                      <p>A closed vocabulary plus a validator makes both ends of the loop reliable: the agent always knows what is allowed, and the reader always sees the same components.</p>
                    </ac:rich-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="expand">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">Open by default</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:rich-text-body>
                      <p>Pass the <code>open</code> attribute to start expanded.</p>
                    </ac:rich-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>Checklists</h2>
                  <p>For action items with optional assignee and due date. Hairline-separated rows, ticks in display face when complete.</p>
                  <ac:task-list>
                    <ac:task>
                      <ac:task-status>complete</ac:task-status>
                      <ac:task-body>
                        <span>Land v0.1 PoC <em>(@dana, due 2026-05-10)</em></span>
                      </ac:task-body>
                    </ac:task>
                    <ac:task>
                      <ac:task-status>complete</ac:task-status>
                      <ac:task-body>
                        <span>Split sources into per-component folders <em>(@dana, due 2026-05-13)</em></span>
                      </ac:task-body>
                    </ac:task>
                    <ac:task>
                      <ac:task-status>complete</ac:task-status>
                      <ac:task-body>
                        <span>Add build pipeline + schema.json <em>(@dana, due 2026-05-14)</em></span>
                      </ac:task-body>
                    </ac:task>
                    <ac:task>
                      <ac:task-status>complete</ac:task-status>
                      <ac:task-body>
                        <span>Editorial redesign + vocabulary expansion <em>(@dana, due 2026-05-16)</em></span>
                      </ac:task-body>
                    </ac:task>
                    <ac:task>
                      <ac:task-status>incomplete</ac:task-status>
                      <ac:task-body>
                        <span>Static-site mode for multi-doc collections <em>(@kim, due 2026-05-25)</em></span>
                      </ac:task-body>
                    </ac:task>
                  </ac:task-list>
                  <h2>Badges</h2>
                  <p>Inline status pills, prefixed with a coloured dot: <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">default</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">info</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Blue</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">success</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Green</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">warn</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Yellow</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">danger</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Red</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">muted</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro></p>
                  <h2>Glossary</h2>
                  <p>Use <code>rd-kv layout="stacked"</code> for a definition list. Terms are set in Fraunces italic, the block is bracketed by hairline rules, rows separated by dotted lines.</p>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Terms used in this document</strong>
                  </p>
                  <table data-layout="default">
                    <colgroup>
                      <col style="width: 200.0px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 760.0px;"/>
                    </colgroup>
                    <tbody>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>richdoc</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>A small framework for AI-authored, human-read HTML documents that uses a closed vocabulary of <code>rd-*</code> custom elements.</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>editorial</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>A design voice borrowed from print publications: hairline rules, oldstyle figures, drop caps, Fraunces display type, restrained colour.</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>closed vocabulary</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>A fixed list of tags that the agent must choose from. New tags require a deliberate authoring decision and a linter update.</td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>optical size</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>A Fraunces variation axis that adjusts letterforms for the size at which they will appear. Set higher (<code>72</code>, <code>144</code>) for display.</td>
                      </tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                  <h2>TL;DR strip</h2>
                  <p>Use <code>&lt;rd-callout type="tldr"&gt;</code> as the first block after the hero. It's a focal summary band with an oversized eyebrow and slightly larger body type than the surrounding prose. See the very top of this page.</p>
                  <h2>Hero</h2>
                  <p>Use <code>rd-hero</code> instead of an ad-hoc <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> + <code>rd-kv</code> opener. It coordinates eyebrow, title, lede, and meta into one display block, and accepts any inline children (e.g. an <code>rd-kv</code>) as an "extras" strip below the meta line. The top of this page demonstrates the full structure.</p>
                  <h2>Banner</h2>
                  <p>Doc-status ribbons sit at the top of the page. Five types:</p>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="panel">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="panel-type">info</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <p>Custom message overrides the default text for the type.</p>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                  <h2>Progress</h2>
                  <p>Linear progress / capacity bar. Accepts a 0..1 decimal, <code>N%</code>, or <code>N/M</code> fraction. The fill animates from 0 to the target on entry.</p>
                  <p><strong>Migration complete:</strong> 78%</p>
                  <p><strong>Tests passing:</strong> 12 / 20</p>
                  <p><strong>Disk used:</strong> 35%</p>
                  <p><strong>Project budget consumed:</strong> 45%</p>
                  <h2>Steps</h2>
                  <p>Numbered procedural steps with a rich body per step. Use this for runbooks, onboarding flows, migration guides. Distinct from <code>rd-timeline</code> (dated history) and <code>rd-checklist</code> (task tracking).</p>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <strong>
                        <s>Drop the assets next to your doc</s>
                      </strong>
                      <p>Run <code>richdoc init docs</code> once in the directory that will hold your <code>.html</code> file.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>
                        <s>Scaffold a new document</s>
                      </strong>
                      <p>Pick a template: <code>richdoc new docs/plan.html -t plan</code>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Write the document</strong>
                      <p>Use only the <code>rd-*</code> tags from the vocabulary. Plain HTML is welcome inside any block.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Validate before sharing</strong>
                      <p>Run <code>richdoc lint docs/plan.html</code>. Linting cleanly is part of "done".</p>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  <h2>Pros and cons</h2>
                  <p>Two-column ✓/✗ grid for decision documents. Distinct from <code>rd-compare</code> — that's a matrix; this is one-sided evaluation of a single option.</p>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="two_equal" ac:breakout-mode="default">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Reasons to adopt</strong>
                  </p>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Already-shipped pattern in the existing vocabulary.</li>
                    <li>No new external dependencies in Phase 1.</li>
                    <li>Linter enforces shape on existing rule engine.</li>
                  </ul>
                </ac:layout-cell>
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Reasons to wait</strong>
                  </p>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Vocabulary grows from ~30 → ~70 entries.</li>
                    <li>Authors must learn the new tags.</li>
                  </ul>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="fixed-width">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <h2>Decision records</h2>
                  <p>ADR-style decision header + rationale. Four statuses control the left rule colour and the status pill.</p>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="decision-list">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-1</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <ac:adf-node type="decision-item">
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-2</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="state">DECIDED</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-content><strong>ADR-014: Adopt Observable Plot for charting</strong> · <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">Accepted</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Green</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> · 2026-05-14 · platform team</ac:adf-content>
                        </ac:adf-node>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                  <p>Bundle size is not a concern for documentation artefacts. Plot's grammar-of-graphics defaults match the editorial voice better than anything we'd hand-roll, and one library covers every chart type plus sparklines.</p>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="decision-list">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-3</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <ac:adf-node type="decision-item">
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-4</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="state">UNDECIDED</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-content><strong>ADR-015: Tufte-style margin notes as a separate component</strong> · <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">Rejected</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Red</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> · 2026-05-14</ac:adf-content>
                        </ac:adf-node>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                  <p>A floating-gutter sidenote (<code>rd-margin</code>) was prototyped but rejected: the page column is centred at 1280px with no reserved gutter, so the negative-margin float overflowed off-screen on 1024–1500px viewports — the exact range the wide-mode media query targeted. <code>rd-cite</code> with <code>rd-ref</code> already covers the related authoring case on every viewport.</p>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="decision-list">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-5</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <ac:adf-node type="decision-item">
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-6</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="state">UNDECIDED</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-content><strong>ADR-007: Original hand-rolled SVG chart approach</strong> · <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">Superseded</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Purple</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> · 2026-02-01</ac:adf-content>
                        </ac:adf-node>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                  <p>Superseded by ADR-014.</p>
                  <ac:adf-extension>
                    <ac:adf-node type="decision-list">
                      <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-7</ac:adf-attribute>
                      <ac:adf-content>
                        <ac:adf-node type="decision-item">
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="local-id">LOCAL-ID-8</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="state">UNDECIDED</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-content><strong>ADR-008: Embedding D3 directly for every chart</strong> · <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1"><ac:parameter ac:name="title">Rejected</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Red</ac:parameter></ac:structured-macro> · 2026-02-15</ac:adf-content>
                        </ac:adf-node>
                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                  <p>Too much per-chart code; Plot covers the same surface with a much smaller author API.</p>
                  <h2>Status updates</h2>
                  <p>Dated reverse-chron entries for changelogs, release notes, status reports. The kind glyph and tint differ between release / change / note.</p>
                  <h3>2026-05-14 — v0.4 — expanded vocabulary</h3>
                  <p>
                    <em>release · platform team</em>
                  </p>
                  <p>Expanded the framework's structural vocabulary — hero, banner, progress, steps, pros/cons, decision records, updates, terminal sessions, API blocks, and rubrics all landed in one release.</p>
                  <h3>2026-05-12 — Schema groups reorganised</h3>
                  <p>
                    <em>change · dana</em>
                  </p>
                  <p>Introduced <em>Decision &amp; planning</em> and <em>Reference</em> groupings in the schema registry. Mirrored in the SKILL.md tag tables.</p>
                  <h3>2026-05-09 — Discovery doc accepted</h3>
                  <p>
                    <em>note</em>
                  </p>
                  <p>The 20-component plan was reviewed and accepted. Implementation phases queued.</p>
                  <h2>FAQ</h2>
                  <p>Question/answer disclosure uses <code>rd-detail variant="question"</code> — questions render in display Fraunces, answers in body Inter, with hairline separators between entries.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="expand">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">Do I have to use richdoc instead of Markdown?</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:rich-text-body>
                      <p>Use Markdown where the renderer might be anything (GitHub, chat, CLI). For documents read in a browser, richdoc gives a much richer presentation with no extra effort.</p>
                    </ac:rich-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="expand">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">Can I add my own tags?</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:rich-text-body>
                      <p>No — the linter will reject unknown <code>rd-*</code> tags. The fixed vocabulary is the point. Extensions ship through a new release.</p>
                    </ac:rich-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="expand">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">Does it work offline?</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:rich-text-body>
                      <p>Yes. The two shipped assets (<code>richdoc.css</code>, <code>richdoc.js</code>) work over <code>file://</code>. Components that need a CDN library (mermaid, KaTeX, charts) degrade to a readable fallback when offline.</p>
                    </ac:rich-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>Shell sessions</h2>
                  <p>Terminal transcripts for runbooks and tutorials. Distinct from <code>rd-code</code> — no syntax highlighting, no copy button, just prompt → output.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="code">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="language">bash</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">~/Projects/agent-skills</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[$ richdoc init docs
          copied richdoc.css → docs/richdoc.css
          copied richdoc.js  → docs/richdoc.js
          $ richdoc new docs/plan.html -t plan
          wrote docs/plan.html (1245 bytes)
          $ richdoc lint docs/plan.html
          {"ok": true, "issues": []}]]></ac:plain-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>API reference</h2>
                  <p>Single-endpoint reference block. Method pill is coloured per HTTP verb; status pills tint themselves based on the 2xx/4xx/5xx class.</p>
                  <table data-layout="default">
                    <colgroup>
                      <col style="width: 200.0px;"/>
                      <col style="width: 760.0px;"/>
                    </colgroup>
                    <tbody>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Endpoint</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                          <p>
                            <ac:structured-macro ac:name="status" ac:schema-version="1">
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="title">POST</ac:parameter>
                              <ac:parameter ac:name="colour">Blue</ac:parameter>
                            </ac:structured-macro>
                            <code>/v1/documents/{id}/validate</code>
                          </p>
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                      <tr>
                        <th>
                          <p>
                            <strong>Description</strong>
                          </p>
                        </th>
