                      </ac:adf-content>
                    </ac:adf-node>
                  </ac:adf-extension>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
              <ac:layout-section ac:type="fixed-width">
                <ac:layout-cell>
                  <h2>Spoilers</h2>
                  <p>Spoilers use <code>rd-detail variant="reveal"</code> — content hides behind an eye-icon button until the reader is ready. Useful for runbook solutions, exam answers, or any "think before you peek" pattern.</p>
                  <ac:structured-macro ac:name="expand">
                    <ac:parameter ac:name="title">Show the answer</ac:parameter>
                    <ac:rich-text-body>
                      <p>The same component can also wrap multiple paragraphs, callouts, or even code blocks — anything that should not be eye-deep on first read.</p>
                      <ac:adf-extension>
                        <ac:adf-node type="panel">
                          <ac:adf-attribute key="panel-type">success</ac:adf-attribute>
                          <ac:adf-content>
                            <p>
                              <strong>Inside the spoiler</strong>
                            </p>
                            <p>Hidden content can include any other component.</p>
                          </ac:adf-content>
                        </ac:adf-node>
                      </ac:adf-extension>
                    </ac:rich-text-body>
                  </ac:structured-macro>
                  <h2>Plain prose, lists, and HTML</h2>
                  <p>You can still use everyday semantic HTML inside any component or directly inside <code>rd-page</code>:</p>
                  <ul>
                    <li><strong>Bold</strong>, <em>italic</em>, <code>code</code>, and <a href="https://example.com">links</a> all work.</li>
                    <li>Standard <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> lists pick up an en-dash bullet.</li>
                    <li>Ordered lists pick up zero-padded numerals in the gutter.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <ol>
                    <li>First step in a sequence.</li>
                    <li>Second step.</li>
                    <li>Third step.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <blockquote>
                    <p>"The best documentation is the kind you actually read." — anonymous</p>
                  </blockquote>
                  <hr/>
                  <p>Horizontal rules separate sections of free-form prose where <code>rd-section</code> would feel heavy.</p>
                  <h2>References</h2>
                  <ol>
                    <li>Tufte, Edward R.. "<a href="https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi">The Visual Display of Quantitative Information</a>". Graphics Press. 2001. Reference work for editorial data graphics; cited in this doc for sparkline philosophy and the footnote-as-aside pattern.</li>
                    <li>Bringhurst, Robert. "The Elements of Typographic Style". Hartley &amp; Marks. 2004.</li>
                    <li>Strunk, William and White, E.B.. "The Elements of Style". 2000.</li>
                    <li>Hochuli, Jost. "Detail in Typography". 2008. Uncited — still appears in the bibliography after the cited works.</li>
                  </ol>
                </ac:layout-cell>
              </ac:layout-section>
            </ac:layout>
        ''',
